A||||1||GodSend||Solve for start problems.||||14||
Z||000000||GodSend||09-09-2000||01:08 PM||||  There is nothing worse then following a thread on a related problem you are having only to find that the answer was never found/posted - SO here is my solution.<P><BR>  I was having problems with warm starts - My 87 Gt would actually start fine, but would simply die if I did not give her any gas for approx. 5-10 seconds.  <P>  In the course of trying to fix this problem I replaced the PVC valve, along with the air filter.  I was trying to diagnose a idle hunting problem at the same time, so my next step was distib cap and rotor.  <P>  I purchased a Wells Gold Cap/Dist , and proceeded to install.  The cap came off no problems, But no matter how hard I yanked in the rotor is would not come off the distributor.  Finally after breaking it into little bits I realized the entire shaft of the distibutor was rusted.<P>  If you look on the inside of the rotor you will notice there is a copper connection which touchs the shaft and conducts current to the 6 spark plug wires - Obviously with all that rust the connection was minimal.<P>  I pulled out the trusty dremel tool, and in less then 2 minutes the shaft was nice and shiney metal.  I proceeded to put the rotor on, attached the wires 1 by 1, put everything together, and said a little prayer.<P> 3 things happened .  <P>1]  To my surprize, my cold starts improved dramatically - Before It was hit or miss if I would have to give my baby a little gas to get it to catch - Now its a nice strong start - every time.<P><BR>2] Hot start problem is fixed..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>3]  My idle hunting improved - rather , it got less.  I used to hunt between 1500-1000 , now im down to 1150-900, so its a nice improvment.<P>  Im pretty sure I have a vac. leek, that will be next weekends project.  For some of you this is probably common sense, but I thought I would share my little experiance to maybe make someones trouble shooting easier - So remember kids, check your dist. shaft and make sure the rotor has a nice solid connection.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.hypernetcommunications.com/revn.gif"> ||||reg||14
Z||000001||StuGood||09-09-2000||02:01 PM||||Thanks for being considerate enough to post your solution.  It may help others, as you mentioned, and it gives me a lift when I read about someone else's success with their Fiero.<P>BTW, between rusty distributor shafts, difficult-to-remove distributors, cooked pickup coils/modules, and gummy, oil-varnished shafts, it makes it pretty obvious (again) that one of the problems is <B>heat</B>.  Too bad they couldn't find a better location for the EGR tube, or at least better materials and insulation for the tube.<P>Oh, well - design compromises <B>do</B> happen...       <P><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-09-2000).]||||reg||10
